1
00:00:03,660 --> 00:00:04,920
So, tell me about your day--

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:06,930
how's it going with the particle detector?

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:08,900
Wow! You remember that?

4
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:10,650
Yeah, I listen to what you say.

5
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:11,910
You're building a particle detector

6
00:00:11,910 --> 00:00:13,790
using superfluid helium.

7
00:00:14,430 --> 00:00:15,650
You know, when you talk like that,

8
00:00:15,650 --> 00:00:18,080
I want to take you right here on this table.

9
00:00:19,030 --> 00:00:20,340
And you know from past experience

10
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:22,920
this table cannot support both our weight.

11
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,540
So, how's the detector going?

12
00:00:25,540 --> 00:00:28,150
Well, it's tricky working with superfluids.

13
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:29,450
Whatever container you put them in,

14
00:00:29,450 --> 00:00:30,980
they kind of creep up the sides

15
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:32,670
and crawl out all on their own.

16
00:00:32,670 --> 00:00:34,340
Kind of like Sheldon's ant farm.

17
00:00:35,110 --> 00:00:37,640
Exactly, except you don't have a lunatic running around,

18
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:40,440
yelling, "I fed you sugar water, why are you biting me?"

19
00:00:42,370 --> 00:00:44,260
Come on, this is boring-- you really want to talk about this?

20
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:44,940
No, I do.

21
00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:46,880
I-I didn't write "superfluid helium"

22
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:48,160
on this napkin for nothing.

23
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:50,950
Okay, well...

24
00:00:51,290 --> 00:00:51,910
This is cool.

25
00:00:51,910 --> 00:00:54,420
Uh, there's a thing called "superfluid vacuum theory",

26
00:00:54,420 --> 00:00:56,450
where empty space is imagined as a superfluid

27
00:00:56,450 --> 00:00:58,020
with all of its qualities--

28
00:00:58,020 --> 00:01:00,240
viscosity, density, surface tension...

29
00:01:03,230 --> 00:01:04,800
Hey, if you're pausing for dramatic effect,

30
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:06,710
I'd keep it moving.

31
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:09,410
No, no.

32
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:14,090
People don't talk about surface tension.

33
00:01:14,090 --> 00:01:17,470
If you imagine our three-space as the surface

34
00:01:17,470 --> 00:01:20,190
of an N-dimensional superfluid bubble...

35
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:22,690
This... is exciting.

36
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:24,450
This is really exciting.

37
00:01:24,450 --> 00:01:26,130
I have to go find Sheldon.

38
00:01:26,490 --> 00:01:28,520
Okay, well, if you find him,

39
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:29,800
use the kitchen island--

40
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:32,520
that coffee table will not support both of you.

41
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,620
Which means a spherical multidimensional superfluid

42
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:40,060
shows the same negative-energy density as space-time.

43
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:42,300
So what do you think? What do you think? So what do you think?

44
00:01:48,620 --> 00:01:50,020
What? Is it wrong?

45
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:51,800
Have you seen it somewhere else?

46
00:01:56,430 --> 00:01:57,890
I know this isn't my area,

47
00:01:57,890 --> 00:01:59,490
and I could never do the math like you can,

48
00:01:59,490 --> 00:02:01,640
but could this be something...?

49
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:03,750
Well...

50
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:08,350
You could have set Newton's gravitational constant to one.

51
00:02:08,350 --> 00:02:10,750
And, ugh, the whole thing reeks of blueberry.

52
00:02:10,750 --> 00:02:13,480
You know I can't stand these scented markers.

53
00:02:14,610 --> 00:02:16,870
No one told you to taste them.

54
00:02:17,910 --> 00:02:20,860
Come on, is-is this good or not?

55
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:23,400
It's good.

56
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:24,910
- Really? - I like it.

57
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:26,190
I think you're on to something.

58
00:02:26,190 --> 00:02:27,460
You do? You're not messing with me?

59
00:02:27,470 --> 00:02:28,940
Not at all.

60
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:31,230
In fact, I have got something

61
00:02:31,230 --> 00:02:32,450
for just such an occasion.

62
00:02:32,450 --> 00:02:33,600
I was starting to think

63
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:35,580
I might never get a chance to give it to you.

64
00:02:36,450 --> 00:02:37,490
Good job.

65
00:02:42,210 --> 00:02:43,640
You're giving me a sticker?

66
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:44,530
Indeed.

67
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:46,540
Not just a sticker.

68
00:02:46,690 --> 00:02:50,310
That is a sticker of a kitty saying, "Me-wow!"

69
00:02:53,050 --> 00:02:54,740
I'm not a preschooler.

70
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:56,240
Fine, I'll take it back.

71
00:02:56,240 --> 00:02:58,150
I earned this! Back off!

72
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:27,060
You're still awake?

73
00:03:27,530 --> 00:03:29,860
For a man whose last observation was

74
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:31,440
our universe may be the surface

75
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:33,720
of a multidimensional supercooled liquid,

76
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:36,770
"You're still awake" seems like quite the sophomore slump.

77
00:03:38,990 --> 00:03:40,550
You worked out all the math.

78
00:03:40,550 --> 00:03:43,160
Oh, I did more than work out all the math.

79
00:03:43,160 --> 00:03:45,450
I wrote a paper.

80
00:03:46,750 --> 00:03:48,740
You wrote a paper on my idea?

81
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:51,050
I wrote a paper on our idea.

82
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:54,400
When did my idea become our idea?

83
00:03:54,410 --> 00:03:56,790
When I mixed it with Sheldony goodness

84
00:03:56,790 --> 00:04:00,430
and cooked it in the Easy-Bake Oven of my mind.

85
00:04:02,550 --> 00:04:03,550
This is good.

86
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:05,940
Our idea is really good.

87
00:04:05,940 --> 00:04:10,280
Well, the lightbulb in this oven is ridiculously bright.

88
00:04:11,550 --> 00:04:13,170
You know, if no one's thought of this, yet,

89
00:04:13,170 --> 00:04:15,040
this could be a big deal.

90
00:04:15,110 --> 00:04:16,400
Only way we'll know for sure is

91
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:18,760
if we post it online to the pre-print server.

92
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:22,220
I have it ready to go, but I wasn't gonna do it without you.

93
00:04:24,360 --> 00:04:26,710
Wow, it's all happening so fast.

94
00:04:27,890 --> 00:04:29,360
Should we just sleep on it?

95
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:31,730
We could, but we always run the risk

96
00:04:31,730 --> 00:04:33,790
of someone else beating us to the punch.

97
00:04:35,030 --> 00:04:36,290
You're sure it's good?

98
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:38,660
My name is right on there with yours.

99
00:04:39,030 --> 00:04:41,670
That is a surefire mark of quality.

100
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:47,450
That might as well say "Directed by Joss Whedon".

101
00:04:49,550 --> 00:04:51,050
Okay, partner, let's do it.

102
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:54,600
Come on.

103
00:04:55,450 --> 00:04:56,730
Click the mouse with me.

104
00:05:05,630 --> 00:05:06,600
Click.

105
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:11,640
Well, we did it.

106
00:05:12,420 --> 00:05:13,980
Yes, we did, my friend.

107
00:05:14,150 --> 00:05:15,090
Is your tongue blue?

108
00:05:15,090 --> 00:05:16,510
I don't want to talk about it.

109
00:05:19,230 --> 00:05:20,880
Okay, movie night-- what do you want to watch?

110
00:05:20,880 --> 00:05:22,860
Whatever happened to that ape movie you were in?

111
00:05:22,870 --> 00:05:24,140
Oh, God.

112
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:25,000
Probably nothing.

113
00:05:25,010 --> 00:05:28,890
I think I saw the director twirling a sign outside the Verizon store.

114
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:30,660
Search for it.

115
00:05:30,660 --> 00:05:32,330
Wha... no. No! Why?

116
00:05:32,330 --> 00:05:33,750
'Cause it would be fun to watch.

117
00:05:33,750 --> 00:05:35,370
It would be humiliating.

118
00:05:35,380 --> 00:05:37,090
Well, now we have two reasons.

119
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:40,160
They have it.

120
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:41,860
Please, can we watch it?

121
00:05:41,860 --> 00:05:42,810
Please?

122
00:05:42,810 --> 00:05:45,120
Fine, but I'm telling you-- it's terrible.

123
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:47,370
- Have you even seen it? - No.

124
00:05:47,410 --> 00:05:49,360
Well, maybe it turned out better than you think.

125
00:05:49,630 --> 00:05:52,200
Bananas, get your fresh bananas.

126
00:05:53,300 --> 00:05:55,140
It really didn't.

127
00:05:59,650 --> 00:06:03,300
Sheldon, this is superfluid helium.

128
00:06:03,880 --> 00:06:04,670
Put this in your mouth,

129
00:06:04,670 --> 00:06:06,770
your tongue will freeze and break off.

130
00:06:08,680 --> 00:06:10,340
Does it smell like blueberries?

131
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:12,280
No.

132
00:06:12,290 --> 00:06:13,580
Then we're probably okay.

133
00:06:14,690 --> 00:06:16,020
Hey, your paper got mentioned

134
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:17,930
on the Quantum Diaries physics blog.

135
00:06:18,350 --> 00:06:19,350
Really?

136
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:20,440
Wh-What'd they say?

137
00:06:20,450 --> 00:06:22,240
Uh, it's basically a summary of the theory,

138
00:06:22,240 --> 00:06:24,020
but there's a bunch of positive comments on the message board.

139
00:06:24,030 --> 00:06:25,060
Let me see, let me see.

140
00:06:26,490 --> 00:06:29,410
One calls it "Insightful and innovative".

141
00:06:29,870 --> 00:06:31,380
We're insightful and innovative.

142
00:06:31,380 --> 00:06:33,960
Ooh, nice to meet you, Mr. Insightful.

143
00:06:33,960 --> 00:06:36,120
Oh, the pleasure is mine, Mr. Innovative.

144
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:39,730
Uh, another one says, "The concept shows

145
00:06:39,730 --> 00:06:42,280
some real out-of-the-box thinking".

146
00:06:42,340 --> 00:06:44,000
Do you hear that, Mr. Out-Of-The?

147
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,360
I do, indeed, Mr. Box-Thinking.

148
00:06:47,720 --> 00:06:48,540
How are you today,

149
00:06:48,540 --> 00:06:51,170
Mr. Can-You-Believe-These-Jack-Asses?

150
00:06:51,810 --> 00:06:53,590
Just dandy, Mr....

151
00:06:53,990 --> 00:06:56,720
I-Wish-I-Was-Better-At-Improv.

152
00:06:57,940 --> 00:07:00,260
Read another one. Okay, okay. Uh...

153
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:02,580
"The analogy between space-time

154
00:07:02,580 --> 00:07:06,230
and a supercooled fluid is either meaningless or... false".

155
00:07:06,910 --> 00:07:09,850
"I wish this blog would devote itself to real science

156
00:07:10,100 --> 00:07:11,890
Instead of wasting our time with crackpot,

157
00:07:11,890 --> 00:07:14,710
wannabe theoreticians in a rush to publish".

158
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:15,960
Who wrote that?

159
00:07:15,970 --> 00:07:17,510
It's anonymous,

160
00:07:17,550 --> 00:07:19,740
and user name "General Relativity."

161
00:07:20,270 --> 00:07:22,700
Well... I'm responding to it.

162
00:07:22,710 --> 00:07:24,970
Uh, don't lower yourself to their level.

163
00:07:24,970 --> 00:07:27,110
Look, I am simply going to defend our work,

164
00:07:27,110 --> 00:07:28,810
scientist to scientist.

165
00:07:28,810 --> 00:07:29,990
And failing that,

166
00:07:29,990 --> 00:07:31,920
suggest that his mother enjoys a string

167
00:07:31,920 --> 00:07:34,080
of both human and non-human lovers.

168
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:37,720
Sheldon, my name's on that paper, too.

169
00:07:37,720 --> 00:07:39,320
There's no upside to doing this.

170
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:40,990
He just left another comment.

171
00:07:40,990 --> 00:07:42,010
What does it say?

172
00:07:42,870 --> 00:07:45,330
"Upon review, I've changed my mind about

173
00:07:45,330 --> 00:07:47,230
The Cooper-Hofstadter hypothesis

174
00:07:47,230 --> 00:07:49,880
That space-time is like a superfluid. In fact,

175
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:52,790
It's inspired me to come up with my own theory.

176
00:07:52,790 --> 00:07:54,830
Maybe space-time is like two clowns

177
00:07:54,830 --> 00:07:56,260
with their heads in a bucket,

178
00:07:56,260 --> 00:07:58,330
much like Cooper and Hofstadter."

179
00:08:00,550 --> 00:08:02,030
Can I respond now?

180
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:03,480
Do it.

181
00:08:03,630 --> 00:08:06,460
You mess with the bull, you get the horns.

182
00:08:07,540 --> 00:08:10,540
I'm about to show this guy just how horny I can be.

183
00:08:15,080 --> 00:08:16,440
Somebody else do it.

184
00:08:19,490 --> 00:08:21,200
Doctor, please help me.

185
00:08:21,250 --> 00:08:23,970
I think I might be turning into a killer gorilla.

186
00:08:25,350 --> 00:08:27,630
Why do you think you're turning into a killer gorilla

187
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:29,360
and not just a regular gorilla?

188
00:08:30,970 --> 00:08:33,290
Because regular gorillas are vegetarians,

189
00:08:33,290 --> 00:08:36,310
and I just bit the fingers off your receptionist!

190
00:08:36,750 --> 00:08:39,600
Okay, we've seen plenty.

191
00:08:39,610 --> 00:08:40,800
No! Give me the remote.

192
00:08:40,810 --> 00:08:42,630
Careful. She'll bite your fingers off.

193
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:45,830
Okay, well, I've been poking around the internet,

194
00:08:45,830 --> 00:08:48,780
and I think I found something we'll enjoy watching even more.

195
00:08:48,780 --> 00:08:49,550
What is it?

196
00:08:49,550 --> 00:08:53,030
Oh, just a video of Bernadette in a beauty pageant.

197
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:54,340
What?!

198
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:55,570
Okay, I learned my lesson.

199
00:08:55,580 --> 00:08:57,230
Making fun of people is wrong!

200
00:08:58,970 --> 00:09:00,040
I haven't learned my lesson.

201
00:09:00,040 --> 00:09:01,260
Play it! Play it!

202
00:09:08,030 --> 00:09:11,420
Hi. I'm Bernadette Maryann Rostenkowski

203
00:09:11,420 --> 00:09:15,660
from Yorba Linda, California.

204
00:09:16,690 --> 00:09:19,110
You look like a talking cupcake!

205
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:21,360
And you should pick me

206
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:24,880
for Miss California Quiznos 1999, because I want to...

207
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:26,910
Tell you what I want, what I really really want

208
00:09:26,910 --> 00:09:28,930
I'll tell you what I want, what I really really want

209
00:09:28,930 --> 00:09:31,150
I wanna, I wanna, I wanna, I wanna

210
00:09:31,150 --> 00:09:33,100
Really, really, really be

211
00:09:33,100 --> 00:09:38,150
Miss California Quiznos 1999!

212
00:09:41,320 --> 00:09:42,850
Play it again! Play it again!

213
00:09:48,660 --> 00:09:50,270
Did he respond yet?

214
00:09:51,210 --> 00:09:52,730
Hmm. No, not yet.

215
00:09:53,460 --> 00:09:55,380
Well, maybe we shouldn't have sunk to his level.

216
00:09:55,380 --> 00:09:56,700
It wasn't that bad.

217
00:09:56,700 --> 00:09:58,890
Read it back one more time.

218
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:03,610
"My good sir, we are neither crackpots nor wannabes.

219
00:10:03,660 --> 00:10:06,370
In fact, we are experts in our fields.

220
00:10:06,370 --> 00:10:08,600
And while you hide behind your anonymity,

221
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:10,670
We stand behind our paper.

222
00:10:10,710 --> 00:10:12,600
And later tonight, your mother".

223
00:10:17,010 --> 00:10:18,520
And you don't think that's too rough?

224
00:10:18,520 --> 00:10:20,680
We're just standing behind her.

225
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:24,300
It's not like we're gonna say "boo" and scare her.

226
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:29,840
How could you not tell us you were in beauty pageants?

227
00:10:29,840 --> 00:10:31,690
'Cause it's embarrassing.

228
00:10:31,690 --> 00:10:33,700
It is; it truly is.

229
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:37,390
Yeah, well...

230
00:10:37,670 --> 00:10:38,650
Amy writes Little House

231
00:10:38,650 --> 00:10:39,910
on the Prairie fan fiction

232
00:10:39,910 --> 00:10:42,010
about herself and posts it on the Internet.

233
00:10:45,310 --> 00:10:46,630
No!

234
00:10:46,630 --> 00:10:49,020
Why? What did I do?!

235
00:10:49,910 --> 00:10:51,900
Sorry, I had to get the spotlight off me,

236
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:54,720
and tearing down other women is part of my pageant training.

237
00:10:55,410 --> 00:10:57,370
Okay. We are reading that right now.

238
00:10:57,370 --> 00:10:58,620
No, please don't.

239
00:10:58,620 --> 00:11:00,390
We got embarrassed tonight. Come on.

240
00:11:00,390 --> 00:11:01,640
But it's personal.

241
00:11:01,640 --> 00:11:03,690
Why? Is it about you and Sheldon?

242
00:11:04,030 --> 00:11:05,130
No.

243
00:11:06,250 --> 00:11:07,790
Oh, my God, it's about her and Sheldon.

244
00:11:07,970 --> 00:11:09,700
It's not about me and Sheldon.

245
00:11:09,700 --> 00:11:13,540
It's about a young woman in the 1800s named Amelia...

246
00:11:14,290 --> 00:11:16,400
and the time-traveling physicist named Cooper

247
00:11:16,410 --> 00:11:17,610
she falls in love with.

248
00:11:18,070 --> 00:11:20,350
- Please show us? - Please?

249
00:11:20,350 --> 00:11:22,920
- No. - You know I'm gonna read it either way.

250
00:11:22,920 --> 00:11:24,480
Good luck finding it.

251
00:11:25,400 --> 00:11:27,480
Amelia and...

252
00:11:27,480 --> 00:11:29,280
the time-trav...

253
00:11:29,280 --> 00:11:30,210
Found it!

254
00:11:32,200 --> 00:11:34,160
"It was just past dawn on the prairie,

255
00:11:34,160 --> 00:11:35,240
and like every morning,

256
00:11:35,240 --> 00:11:37,300
Amelia prepared to do her chores.

257
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:40,200
Except something about this morning felt different."

258
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:41,480
Why?

259
00:11:43,810 --> 00:11:46,200
Why did it feel different?

260
00:11:47,940 --> 00:11:50,640
"Maybe it was the first whisper of winter in the air,

261
00:11:50,640 --> 00:11:53,210
o maybe it was the unconscious handsome man

262
00:11:53,210 --> 00:11:55,160
with porcelain skin and curious clothing

263
00:11:55,160 --> 00:11:57,810
she was about to discover lying in the field.

264
00:11:57,810 --> 00:12:00,700
A man who would open her mind to new possibilities

265
00:12:00,720 --> 00:12:03,720
and her body to new feelings."

266
00:12:10,460 --> 00:12:13,610
You know, there was a time when I was alone and had no friends.

267
00:12:14,100 --> 00:12:15,490
I'm starting to miss that.

268
00:12:18,710 --> 00:12:20,710
He still hasn't responded.

269
00:12:20,710 --> 00:12:22,010
What's taking him so long?

270
00:12:22,010 --> 00:12:23,650
Do you really want him to write back?

271
00:12:23,650 --> 00:12:26,180
Yeah, I do. No matter how he responds,

272
00:12:26,180 --> 00:12:30,160
I am going to destroy him with a picture of a bored cat

273
00:12:30,160 --> 00:12:33,150
saying, "Oh, really?"

274
00:12:37,970 --> 00:12:38,940
Me-wow.

275
00:12:41,910 --> 00:12:42,850
What are you doing?

276
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:45,450
I've, uh, created some other user accounts

277
00:12:45,450 --> 00:12:48,120
so I can post positive comments about their paper.

278
00:12:48,120 --> 00:12:50,990
"This wee li'l bairn of a theory

279
00:12:50,990 --> 00:12:52,870
nearly blew my kilt off"?

280
00:12:52,870 --> 00:12:56,240
No. You have to read it how Dr. Angus McDoogal

281
00:12:56,240 --> 00:12:58,830
of the University of Edinburgh would.

282
00:12:58,990 --> 00:13:00,770
"This wee li'l bairn of a theory

283
00:13:00,770 --> 00:13:02,620
nearly blew me kilt off."

284
00:13:05,710 --> 00:13:07,010
He wrote back!

285
00:13:07,320 --> 00:13:11,430
"Cooper and Hofstadter resorting to juvenile attempts at humor

286
00:13:11,430 --> 00:13:15,010
Is proof they have nothing to back up their ridiculous paper.

287
00:13:15,010 --> 00:13:16,570
It should come as no surprise

288
00:13:16,570 --> 00:13:20,230
Given they work at Cal Tech, essentially a technical school,

289
00:13:20,230 --> 00:13:23,730
where even the physicists are basically engineers."

290
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:25,950
"Engineers"!

291
00:13:27,490 --> 00:13:30,270
Do you know how insulting that is?

292
00:13:30,980 --> 00:13:31,870
Yes.

293
00:13:33,380 --> 00:13:35,480
Guys, this person's just going out of their way

294
00:13:35,480 --> 00:13:36,750
to get a rise out of you.

295
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:38,590
Yeah, but it's still so aggravating.

296
00:13:38,600 --> 00:13:40,970
Yeah, well, all the other comments said really nice things.

297
00:13:40,970 --> 00:13:42,730
- Focus on those. - Yeah.

298
00:13:42,730 --> 00:13:47,490
Dr. Dmitri Plancovik of Moscow University said,

299
00:13:47,800 --> 00:13:49,000
"Dis paper great.

300
00:13:49,010 --> 00:13:51,050
I love it more than 'wodka.".

301
00:13:53,430 --> 00:13:55,410
See? Better with the accent.

302
00:13:56,500 --> 00:13:58,540
And... send.

303
00:13:58,680 --> 00:13:59,600
What did you write?

304
00:13:59,600 --> 00:14:01,220
I'm done hiding from bullies.

305
00:14:01,230 --> 00:14:02,950
I'm taking this into the real world.

306
00:14:02,950 --> 00:14:03,710
What does that mean?

307
00:14:03,710 --> 00:14:05,650
I told him we'll meet him face-to-face

308
00:14:05,660 --> 00:14:06,990
anytime, anywhere.

309
00:14:06,990 --> 00:14:09,000
Are you crazy? You don't know who this person is.

310
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:10,580
- Delete that! - Come on, Sheldon!

311
00:14:10,580 --> 00:14:12,970
Oh, okay, okay, calm down.

312
00:14:15,890 --> 00:14:16,900
It's him!

313
00:14:17,470 --> 00:14:19,210
He's trying to video chat.

314
00:14:23,450 --> 00:14:25,850
Perhaps I shouldn't have taken this into the real world.

315
00:14:26,970 --> 00:14:29,000
Oh, really?

316
00:14:31,580 --> 00:14:34,280
'"Time travel? I don't understand,' "Said Amelia.

317
00:14:34,280 --> 00:14:35,600
Cooper stared at her.

318
00:14:35,600 --> 00:14:38,590
'Which rd don't you understand, time or travel?'"

319
00:14:39,680 --> 00:14:43,190
Wow, even in your fantasies Sheldon's kind of exhausting.

320
00:14:44,840 --> 00:14:46,250
He's like that in the beginning,

321
00:14:46,250 --> 00:14:47,910
so she can change him.

322
00:14:47,910 --> 00:14:49,440
It's called good writing.

323
00:14:50,730 --> 00:14:52,130
And wishful thinking.

324
00:14:53,540 --> 00:14:54,680
"It stung Amelia "

325
00:14:54,680 --> 00:14:55,810
When he spoke to her this way.

326
00:14:55,810 --> 00:14:57,240
In her little one-room schoolhouse,

327
00:14:57,240 --> 00:14:58,830
she was always the smartest student,

328
00:14:58,830 --> 00:15:00,990
regularly besting the boys in her class,

329
00:15:00,990 --> 00:15:02,810
but this was no boy in front of her--

330
00:15:02,810 --> 00:15:04,090
"This was a man.

331
00:15:04,090 --> 00:15:04,920
Here we go.

332
00:15:06,300 --> 00:15:07,320
"Cooper told Amelia

333
00:15:07,320 --> 00:15:08,970
about all the strange and incredible things

334
00:15:08,970 --> 00:15:10,710
the future would hold, like computers

335
00:15:10,710 --> 00:15:12,200
and living past 30.

336
00:15:12,930 --> 00:15:14,820
He asked her if she had any questions.

337
00:15:14,830 --> 00:15:16,090
All she longed to ask was

338
00:15:16,090 --> 00:15:18,390
if his heart was beating as fast as hers,

339
00:15:18,390 --> 00:15:20,810
but she was too afraid to hear the answer."

340
00:15:20,960 --> 00:15:22,240
Oh, Amelia.

341
00:15:22,240 --> 00:15:24,180
"So instead she asked if, in the future,

342
00:15:24,180 --> 00:15:26,510
montana ever became a state."

343
00:15:30,950 --> 00:15:33,510
In the 1800s that was considered flirting.

344
00:15:36,320 --> 00:15:39,480
I'm so sick of people being mean on the Internet.

345
00:15:39,730 --> 00:15:42,280
Well, I think the anonymity makes everyone feel like

346
00:15:42,280 --> 00:15:44,910
they can say things they'd never say to your face.

347
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:46,750
Interesting.

348
00:15:47,360 --> 00:15:48,680
I can't think of a single thing

349
00:15:48,680 --> 00:15:50,590
I wouldn't say to someone's face.

350
00:15:52,700 --> 00:15:54,590
Never noticed that about you.

351
00:15:56,320 --> 00:15:59,210
You know what? A-At least you guys did something.

352
00:15:59,710 --> 00:16:01,340
You know, you-you had a theory,

353
00:16:01,340 --> 00:16:02,130
you wrote a paper,

354
00:16:02,130 --> 00:16:04,660
you made an actual contribution.

355
00:16:04,740 --> 00:16:08,340
All guys like this do is just stand on other people's work.

356
00:16:09,520 --> 00:16:10,540
He's right.

357
00:16:12,390 --> 00:16:14,830
I say we call this person back.

358
00:16:15,330 --> 00:16:17,060
We've got no reason to hide.

359
00:16:19,260 --> 00:16:21,210
All right, do it. Call him.

360
00:16:21,340 --> 00:16:23,720
Let's find out what this loser's ever accomplished.

361
00:16:27,510 --> 00:16:28,650
Click it with me.

362
00:16:30,580 --> 00:16:32,330
One, two, three...

363
00:16:32,330 --> 00:16:33,430
- Click. - Click.

364
00:16:35,970 --> 00:16:37,930
Well, hello there.

365
00:16:39,250 --> 00:16:40,610
Professor Hawking?

366
00:16:40,730 --> 00:16:44,370
Oh, brother, you should see the look on your faces.

367
00:16:46,870 --> 00:16:48,880
You really didn't like our paper?

368
00:16:48,920 --> 00:16:51,440
I like your paper very much.

369
00:16:51,670 --> 00:16:54,010
The premise is intriguing.

370
00:16:54,550 --> 00:16:56,750
Then why are you attacking us?

371
00:16:56,810 --> 00:17:00,020
If you were sitting in a chair for 40 years,

372
00:17:00,020 --> 00:17:01,840
you'd get bored, too.

373
00:17:04,430 --> 00:17:05,390
Anyway,

374
00:17:05,390 --> 00:17:06,380
got to go.

375
00:17:06,380 --> 00:17:10,520
I promised to help the neighbor kid with his math homework.

376
00:17:11,940 --> 00:17:12,890
Ciao.

377
00:17:16,580 --> 00:17:18,860
Stephen Hawking liked our paper.

378
00:17:20,310 --> 00:17:22,620
Said the premise is intriguing.

379
00:17:23,700 --> 00:17:25,100
Good to see you again,

380
00:17:25,100 --> 00:17:27,330
Mr. Stephen-Hawking-Liked-Our-Paper.

381
00:17:29,800 --> 00:17:32,880
And you as well, Mr. Our-Premise-Is-Intriguing.

382
00:17:34,250 --> 00:17:37,390
How do you do, Mr. I'll- Admit-That's-Pretty-Cool?

383
00:17:39,230 --> 00:17:41,430
Yeah, you keep setting me up for failure.

384
00:17:44,900 --> 00:17:45,800
"With a heavy heart,

385
00:17:45,800 --> 00:17:48,430
Amelia stood before the newly repaired time machine.

386
00:17:48,430 --> 00:17:50,990
She regretted giving Cooper the part he needed."

387
00:17:50,990 --> 00:17:54,190
Because she wanted him to give her the part she needed.

388
00:17:56,310 --> 00:17:57,620
- Okay, that's enough. - What?

389
00:17:57,620 --> 00:18:00,210
No! I really want to know what happens--

390
00:18:00,210 --> 00:18:00,890
and Bernadette

391
00:18:00,890 --> 00:18:03,270
really, really, really wants to know what happens.

392
00:18:05,150 --> 00:18:06,810
You're just making fun of me.

393
00:18:06,810 --> 00:18:08,510
I was just kidding. I'm sorry.

394
00:18:08,510 --> 00:18:09,990
And the story's really good!

395
00:18:09,990 --> 00:18:11,960
No, it is! Does he stay?

396
00:18:11,960 --> 00:18:14,920
Do they kiss? Does she find out about Montana?

397
00:18:16,780 --> 00:18:17,830
Please?

398
00:18:18,660 --> 00:18:19,530
Fine.

399
00:18:21,870 --> 00:18:24,020
"As Cooper prepared to depart,

400
00:18:24,040 --> 00:18:26,270
Tears filled Amelia's eyes.

401
00:18:26,890 --> 00:18:30,610
he took her hand in his and said, 'I can't stay,

402
00:18:30,650 --> 00:18:32,460
but I will never forget you.'

403
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,320
He brushed his fingers against her cheek,

404
00:18:35,390 --> 00:18:37,500
then quickly stepped into the machine.

405
00:18:38,100 --> 00:18:40,280
'Please don't go,' she whispered.

406
00:18:40,690 --> 00:18:42,180
But it was too late.

407
00:18:42,190 --> 00:18:44,130
The engine hummed to life."

408
00:18:44,480 --> 00:18:46,260
But they didn't even kiss.

409
00:18:46,990 --> 00:18:48,260
"She turned away,

410
00:18:48,260 --> 00:18:49,670
wiping her eyes.

411
00:18:50,040 --> 00:18:51,300
She couldn't bear to watch

412
00:18:51,300 --> 00:18:54,380
her one chance at true love disappear forever.

413
00:18:54,770 --> 00:18:55,380
Then...

414
00:18:55,380 --> 00:18:57,560
she felt a strong hand on her shoulder

415
00:18:57,560 --> 00:18:59,070
spin her around.

416
00:18:59,340 --> 00:19:00,730
it was Cooper."

417
00:19:01,320 --> 00:19:02,450
Yes!

418
00:19:04,980 --> 00:19:07,180
"'What about the future?' asked Amelia.

419
00:19:07,650 --> 00:19:10,280
He looked deeply into her eyes and whispered,

420
00:19:10,600 --> 00:19:12,990
'There is no future without you.'

421
00:19:13,690 --> 00:19:15,330
He pulled her in close.

422
00:19:15,780 --> 00:19:18,050
She began to tremble all over.

423
00:19:18,770 --> 00:19:20,450
She felt his warm breath..."

424
00:19:20,450 --> 00:19:21,930
You will not believe what Stephen Hawking just said.

425
00:19:21,930 --> 00:19:23,860
- Get out!  - Not now!

426
00:19:35,160 --> 00:19:37,570
Is the water warm enough?

427
00:19:37,970 --> 00:19:41,310
Given the fact that you took the time to build a wood fire,

428
00:19:41,310 --> 00:19:43,690
draw the water from the well and heat it,

429
00:19:43,690 --> 00:19:46,420
it would be rude to complain.

430
00:19:47,600 --> 00:19:50,120
But since you asked, it's a little nippy.

431
00:19:51,550 --> 00:19:53,500
I can fix that.

432
00:19:58,550 --> 00:20:02,870
I couldn't help but notice your unusual undergarments.

433
00:20:03,370 --> 00:20:05,020
They're not undergarments.

434
00:20:05,060 --> 00:20:07,500
They're Underoos.

435
00:20:08,770 --> 00:20:10,850
Where I come from, they're known as

436
00:20:10,860 --> 00:20:13,440
"Underwear that's fun to wear."

437
00:20:15,120 --> 00:20:18,320
And what's the significance of the spider?

438
00:20:18,320 --> 00:20:21,140
Oh, that represents Spider-Man.

439
00:20:21,140 --> 00:20:23,800
He does whatever a spider can.

440
00:20:26,400 --> 00:20:29,040
There's a lot of rhyming in the future, isn't there?

441
00:20:29,820 --> 00:20:30,850
You're right.

442
00:20:30,850 --> 00:20:33,710
This is even weirder than I thought.

443
00:20:34,100 --> 00:20:35,160
You want me to stop reading?

444
00:20:35,160 --> 00:20:37,250
Are you kidding? No, no.

445
00:20:38,560 --> 00:20:41,640
"As he stood for Amelia to dry him..."

446
00:20:46,470 --> 00:20:47,850
So, tell me, Cooper...

447
00:20:49,020 --> 00:20:53,160
are the ways of physical love different in the future?

448
00:20:53,160 --> 00:20:54,470
Yeah, okay, I'm good.

